The '''Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Act''' is a [[Parliament of Burawa|Burawi]] law enacted in 2022 that established the '''Transportation Workers Displacement Fund''', a financial support mechanism for transportation sector workers displaced by automation. The fund provides fully funded salaries and pensions for workers in positions that have been explicitly designated for phase-out as part of a planned transition to automated operations.

The Act initially covered only passenger rail operators, whose positions were scheduled to be phased out by 2035 as part of the transition to automated train operations. The Act included provisions allowing future amendments to extend the fund's coverage to additional roles within the transportation sector.

== Provisions ==

The Act established a displacement fund with the following core provisions:

The fund covers workers in positions that have been explicitly designated for phase-out as part of a documented automation programme. To be eligible for the fund's protections, a worker must have been employed in a covered position for a period falling within a defined eligibility window: between 20 years before and at least 4 years prior to the planned phase-out date of the position. Workers within this eligibility window receive fully funded salary payments and pension contributions from the fund following their displacement.

The 20-year and 4-year thresholds were designed to ensure that the fund covered workers with substantial tenure in the affected positions while also protecting more recent hires who had entered the workforce with a reasonable expectation of continued employment. The 4-year minimum tenure requirement excluded workers who had joined the position shortly before the phase-out was announced, on the basis that such workers had entered employment with knowledge of the impending automation.

The fund's coverage is limited to positions for which a specific phase-out timeline has been established. This requirement ensures that the fund's obligations are tied to defined, time-bound automation programmes rather than open-ended or speculative displacement scenarios.

== Covered categories ==

=== Passenger rail operators ===

At the time of enactment, the Act specified only passenger rail operators as a covered category. Passenger rail operators were scheduled to be phased out by 2035 as part of the national transition to automated train operations. The 2035 phase-out date provided a clear displacement horizon for the fund, with the eligibility window covering workers employed in passenger rail operations between 2002 and 2031.

The inclusion of passenger rail operators reflected the advanced state of automated train technology in [[Burawa]] and the government's commitment to a managed transition for affected workers. The fund was designed to ensure that rail operators with long tenure in the position would receive continued financial support following displacement, rather than being required to seek alternative employment without compensation.

=== Amendment mechanism ===

The Act included provisions allowing future amendments to extend the fund's coverage to additional roles within the transportation sector. Any expansion of coverage would require separate legislation to add new categories of workers to the fund's eligible positions. This amendment mechanism was the basis for the [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Addendum]] introduced in August 2026, which sought to add taxiworkers to the fund's covered categories.

== Legislative history ==

=== Drafting and introduction ===

The Act was developed in late 2021 and early 2022 by the office of [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] [[Marik Sahamin]] in coordination with [[BuRail|Burawa Railways]] (BuRail), the state-owned national rail operator. The [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]] had mandated the modernisation of public transit infrastructure, and by 2021, BuRail had developed an internal timeline for the full automation of passenger rail operations by 2035. The displacement fund was conceived as a companion measure to address the labour impact of the automation programme.

The bill was introduced in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] on 14 February 2022. At the time, the Sahamin government was managing the final phase of the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Burawa|COVID-19 pandemic]], with restrictions being progressively lifted through the first half of the year. The bill's introduction coincided with a period of elevated inflation (2021–2023) and was followed later in the year by the [[2022 Sabia operation]] and the [[2022 Artificial Alignment Bill]], making the 2022 legislative session one of the most consequential of Sahamin's tenure.

[[Prime Minister of Burawa|Prime Minister]] [[Iacob Naq]] of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] endorsed the bill on behalf of the governing coalition, noting that the displacement of rail workers was a direct consequence of state policy and that the government bore a corresponding obligation to provide for affected employees.

=== Lower House proceedings ===

The bill was referred to committee in March 2022 for review. Committee hearings received testimony from BuRail executives, representatives of the Burawi Rail Workers' Union, and officials from the Ministry of Labour. The committee reported the bill without amendment on 12 April 2022, though committee members from the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] and the [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]] appended dissenting statements expressing opposite concerns — the Industrial Party arguing that the fund was fiscally unnecessary, and the Communist Party arguing that the eligibility thresholds were too restrictive.

Floor debate in the Lower House commenced on 19 April 2022. The debate centred on three principal issues: the salary and pension funding model, the four-year tenure requirement, and the amendment mechanism.

[[Liban Qaasim]], [[Chairman of the Industrial Party of Burawa|chairman of the Industrial Party]], led opposition to the funding model. Qaasim argued that displaced rail workers, as salaried state employees, could be reassigned to comparable positions within the public sector through existing reworking programmes, and that a dedicated fund providing ongoing salary and pension payments was unnecessary and fiscally irresponsible. He proposed an amendment to replace the salary and pension provisions with a retraining and reassignment programme administered by the Ministry of Labour. The amendment was defeated on 3 May 2022 by a vote of 251 to 545.

Representatives of the [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]] and the [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]] criticised the four-year tenure requirement as excessively restrictive. [[Cabdullahi Rooble]], Communist Party Lower House member for [[Badalga]], argued that the threshold excluded workers who had entered the rail workforce in the years immediately preceding the phase-out announcement, and that all rail workers facing displacement should receive the fund's protections regardless of length of service. The Communist Party proposed an amendment to eliminate the four-year threshold entirely, leaving only the 20-year lookback provision. This amendment was defeated on 3 May 2022 by a vote of 189 to 607.

Members of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] expressed concerns about the amendment mechanism, arguing that it could be used to expand the fund's obligations to additional categories of workers without sufficient scrutiny. [[Hancil Thea]], National Party member for the [[Dhulsarren]] region, proposed an amendment requiring that any future expansion of the fund's coverage be subject to a separate parliamentary vote with a minimum 60 per cent supermajority in both chambers. The amendment was defeated on 3 May 2022 by a vote of 278 to 518, with the government arguing that the existing requirement for separate legislation to amend the fund was a sufficient safeguard.

The [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] (CSU) broadly supported the bill. [[Giorgis Nuur]], CSU Lower House member, cited Catholic social teaching on the obligations of employers — particularly the state — to provide for workers displaced by technological change. Nuur argued that the fund was consistent with the principle of solidarity and the state's duty of care toward its employees. The CSU's support was noted as unusual, as the party frequently opposed Sahamin's legislative agenda on other matters.

The bill passed its third reading in the Lower House on 17 May 2022 by a vote of 569 to 227.

=== Senate proceedings ===

The bill was transmitted to the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] on 20 May 2022 and referred to committee for review. Senate committee hearings were briefer than those in the Lower House, as the substantive debate had already been conducted. The committee reported the bill without amendment on 14 June 2022.

Senate debate on 28 June 2022 was shorter than the Lower House debate. The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], which held a majority in the Senate, strongly supported the bill as a component of Sahamin's transportation modernisation agenda. The [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] Senate caucus endorsed the bill with limited dissent. The Industrial Party Senate caucus reiterated the objections raised in the Lower House but did not propose additional amendments.

The bill passed the Senate on 12 July 2022 by a vote of 172 to 58.

=== Enactment ===

The bill received the assent of [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] [[Marik Sahamin]] on 20 July 2022 and was enacted as law. The Act took effect on 1 August 2022. The fund began accepting registrations from eligible passenger rail operators in September 2022.

The enactment received limited public attention, as media coverage in July 2022 was dominated by the [[2022 Sabia operation]], which had occurred in the preceding weeks and generated significant domestic and international debate. The displacement fund was noted in retrospective analyses as one of several major legislative achievements of Sahamin's government in 2022, alongside the Artificial Alignment Bill, though it received less public scrutiny than the military intervention.

== Reception ==

The Act was generally well received by labour organisations and transportation sector unions. The Burawi Rail Workers' Union endorsed the fund as an appropriate response to the automation programme, though some union representatives argued that the four-year threshold should have been lower. The [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]] noted the fund's establishment at the time but did not campaign for its extension to taxiworkers, as no autonomous vehicle deployment was anticipated in 2022.

Budget analysts raised concerns about the long-term cost of the fund, particularly given the 13-year period between enactment and the 2035 phase-out date. The Ministry of Finance estimated that the fund's total obligation would depend on the number of eligible workers who registered and the duration of their salary and pension payments following displacement. The government committed to publishing annual reports on the fund's financial position.

The amendment mechanism was noted by political commentators as a potentially significant feature of the Act, as it allowed future governments to extend the fund's coverage to additional categories of transportation workers without establishing an entirely new legislative framework. This feature became consequential in August 2026, when the [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Addendum]] was introduced to extend coverage to taxiworkers.

== Relationship to the August 2026 Taxiworkers Union protest ==

{{main|2026 Taxiworkers Union protest|Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Addendum}}

The Transportation Workers Displacement Fund became the subject of national attention in August 2026, when the [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]] organised a nationwide work stoppage in response to the deployment of the [[WhA Locus]] autonomous robotaxi. The [[Government of Burawa]], led by [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] [[Muna Xasan]], introduced the [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Addendum]] to extend the fund's coverage to taxiworkers.

The Addendum failed in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] on 12 August 2026. A central argument raised against the Addendum was that the fund was designed for positions with a defined phase-out timeline — as in the case of passenger rail operators facing a legislated 2035 deadline — and that no comparable timeline existed for taxiworkers. The initial WhA Locus deployment comprised 150 vehicles in a single metropolitan area, with no announced schedule for a broader phase-out of conventional taxi services. Opponents argued that adding taxiworkers to the fund would expand its obligations without a corresponding displacement horizon, undermining the fund's design principle of tying coverage to planned, time-bound automation programmes.

